# Interactive Spectrogram Analyzer
This notebook demonstrates an interactive spectrogram visualization tool for time-resolved spectroscopy data. It combines compiled color functions, bandpass filtering, and dynamic controls to enable real-time exploration of spectral features across time and frequency domains.

## Setting up
Import experimental trace data and define helper functions for signal processing

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Interactive Spectrogram Analyzer
This notebook demonstrates an interactive spectrogram visualization tool for time-resolved spectroscopy data. It combines compiled color functions, bandpass filtering, and dynamic controls to enable real-time exploration of spectral features across time and frequency domains.

## Setting up
Import experimental trace data and define helper functions for signal processing

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
trace = NotebookRead[NotebookStore["arced-cae"]];
trace = {trace[[All,1]], trace[[All,2]]/Max[trace[[All,2]]]}//Transpose;
makeYScale[offset_, window_:50, samples_:2500] := Table[(i -1) (*FB[*)((667.30074)(*,*)/(*,*)(samples))(*]FB*) , {i, offset+1, offset + window+1, (*FB[*)((window)(*,*)/(*,*)(10))(*]FB*)}];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### Compiled color mapping function
Compile a custom color function to C for maximum performance when rendering the spectrogram raster. This uses a polynomial-based color mapping optimized for spectroscopic data visualization.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Compiled color mapping function
Compile a custom color function to C for maximum performance when rendering the spectrogram raster. This uses a polynomial-based color mapping optimized for spectroscopic data visualization.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
cfo = Compile[{{list, _Real, 2}}, Map[ {0.4466027122519205` -2.210626749714261` #+6.865407441039864` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))-4.26737262359006` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)3](*]SpB*)), 0.04945207212813584` +1.7555703791510282` Sin[#]-1.4296264911699414` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)3](*]SpB*)), 0.5272670986831703` +2.161471735764603` #+2.546707325549301` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))-49.85096452681392` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)3](*]SpB*))+112.23378888701693` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)4](*]SpB*))-97.99657163556687` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)5](*]SpB*))+30.511586878015482` ((*SpB[*)Power[#1(*|*),(*|*)6](*]SpB*))}&, list, {2}], RuntimeOptions->"Speed", CompilationTarget->"C"];

